          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Flooring for Basement",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-flooring-for-basement/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best flooring for a basement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most finished basements, rigid-core vinyl plank with an attached pad is the best balance of moisture tolerance, comfort, looks and cost. The plank is waterproof and floats over the slab. Choose a product rated for below-grade installation, ideally with a 20 to 30 mil wear layer, and follow the maker's vapor barrier instructions."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a subfloor in my basement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not always. If the slab tests dry and is not especially cold, vinyl plank can go directly over a vapor barrier. A raised dimpled subfloor helps where the slab is slightly damp, and a foam-backed insulated panel helps where it is dry but cold. Both raise the floor, so check door and stair clearances first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you put laminate flooring in a basement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but we would choose vinyl instead. Most laminate has a wood-fiber core that swells if water reaches it through seams or from below. Some newer laminates carry water-resistance claims, but a basement leak that a vinyl plank shrugs off can ruin a laminate floor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is porcelain tile good for a basement floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, it is the most water-tolerant finished floor you can install, absorbing no more than 0.5 percent water by ANSI definition. The downsides are cost, installation labor and a cold, hard surface. An uncoupling membrane helps prevent cracks, and radiant heat or rugs handle the cold."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is epoxy a good basement floor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Epoxy is a good, low-cost choice for a dry slab used as a gym, laundry or workshop. It is not suited to slabs with a moisture problem, because vapor from below can lift the coating. Rust-Oleum's data sheet for its basement kit says this explicitly, so test the slab first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What flooring should I avoid in a basement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Avoid solid hardwood and wall-to-wall carpet with a pad. Solid wood cups and swells with basement humidity swings, and carpet pad soaks up water and hides leaks. Laminate is a weaker choice than vinyl for the same reason: its core can swell if it gets wet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I keep a basement floor from getting moldy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fix any water entry first, run a dehumidifier to keep humidity around 45 to 50 percent, use inorganic flooring materials, follow the vapor barrier instructions and leave the perimeter expansion gap. Mold needs moisture and something organic to feed on, so remove both."
