          <a href="#carpet-wood" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Carpet and Wood</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Installation Details</a>
          <a href="#budget" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. What It Costs</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          For most finished basements, a rigid-core vinyl plank with an attached pad, laid over a dry slab, is the floor to buy. If the slab is cold or a little damp, put a raised subfloor panel underneath it. Everything else on this list solves a narrower problem, and the first thing to settle is not which floor you like but how much water your concrete is giving off.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked These Floors
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not install seven floors in a test basement and flood it, and we are not going to pretend otherwise. This guide is built from manufacturer data sheets, installation guides and retailer listings, and every number is attributed to where it came from. Where two sources disagree, as they do on the R-value of an insulated subfloor panel or the thickness of a DRIcore panel, we say so rather than picking the more flattering figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            The selection criterion that matters most in a basement is not looks or price. It is how a floor behaves when moisture rises through the slab, because below-grade concrete is in contact with soil on the other side and passes water vapor upward all year. A floor that tolerates that vapor, or a system that lets it escape, lasts. A floor that traps it against organic material such as wood, paper-faced backing or carpet pad grows mold and fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            So we sorted the options by moisture tolerance first. Inorganic, waterproof surfaces such as vinyl plank, porcelain tile, epoxy and rubber sit at the top. Raised or insulated subfloor panels come next, because they change the conditions under any finished floor. Carpet and wood come last, because they only work when the slab is reliably dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Within each category we chose products that are widely sold, list a below-grade or basement use, and publish enough specification detail to compare. We left out products whose specs we could not confirm, and we flag where a figure comes only from a single retailer. Our <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/">vinyl plank flooring guide</a> covers the plank category in more depth if that is where you are headed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Test the Slab Before You Choose a Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful thing you can do before buying basement flooring costs almost nothing. Tape a 2 foot square of clear plastic sheeting to the bare slab on all four edges and leave it for 24 to 48 hours. If droplets form under the plastic, moisture is coming up through the concrete. If they form on top, the room air is humid and condensing on a cold slab. Both are problems, but they have different fixes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture from below points toward waterproof floors and raised subfloors, and sometimes toward fixing the source first. If water is actually entering at the joint between floor and wall, or through cracks, no floor will save you, and the order of work is to stop the water, then dry the space, then floor it. Our guides to <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/">water coming through a basement floor</a> and <a href="/basement-moisture-control/">basement moisture control</a> walk through those fixes, and active leaks in walls usually call for <a href="/best-hydraulic-cement/">hydraulic cement</a> before anything else.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensation on top of the plastic points toward humidity control. Basements commonly run humid in summer because cool surfaces sit below the dew point of the incoming air. A properly sized dehumidifier, set somewhere around 45 to 50 percent relative humidity, often fixes this entirely, and our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier picks</a> cover sizing. Running one before and after the floor goes in is cheap insurance for any floor on this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Manufacturers set their own moisture limits, often using calcium chloride or in-slab relative humidity tests, and a flooring warranty may depend on meeting them. The plastic sheet test tells you whether there is a problem; the manufacturer's test tells you whether you are inside their warranty. If you are spending real money on a finished floor, it is worth running the test the manufacturer names.
          </p>
        </div>

